.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
Unsetting a nonexistent alias is not an error.
This provides idempotent semantics for cleanup operations.
.IP \(bu 2
Removing an alias does not affect variables, functions, or traps with
the same name.
Each namespace is independent.
.IP \(bu 2
Unlike variables, aliases cannot have a \f[CR]readonly\f[R] attribute in
standard bash, so unset operations on aliases should always succeed if
the alias exists.
.IP \(bu 2
The built\-in aliases (if any were defined during session initialization
via \f[CR]\-\-init\f[R] or profile scripts) can be removed just like
user\-defined aliases.
.IP \(bu 2
The function uses the v2 binary protocol internally.
The STATE channel request includes \f[CR]\(dqop\(dq: \(dqunset\(dq\f[R],
\f[CR]\(dqns\(dq: \(dqalias\(dq\f[R], and \f[CR]\(dqname\(dq\f[R]
fields.
.IP \(bu 2
A single \f[CR]bc_client_t\f[R] must not be used concurrently from
multiple threads.
